{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at f116097aacda9aee74ccdac0d6d762f9fe900f2f, the candidate for part two of the kernel admission gaps: explicit back-pressure.\n\nThe design gives the sequencer five named refusals, one of which is back-pressure — at capacity, refuse before chaining, cheaply, and say so. Neither half existed. Submitter.Submit queued every caller on its mutex with no bound, and the only overload outcome was an exhausted CAS retry limit returned as a bare error indistinguishable from any other failure.\n\nMaxQueueDepth bounds the submissions inside Submit at once. Over the bound, Submit returns ErrBackPressure after one atomic add taken before the lock, so a refused submission neither waits behind the queue it was refused for nor writes a payload tree it will not use. Zero leaves the queue unbounded, which is what every existing deployment has today. Both exhausted-retry sites, in submit and in Rotate, now wrap the same sentinel, so a caller distinguishes overload with errors.Is rather than by matching message text.\n\nFull gates at this exact head: vet 0, build 0, test 0, nineteen packages, no failures.\n\nFour tests, and I mutated the implementation to confirm each pins something rather than passing decoratively. Deleting the capacity gate fails the test. Moving the gate after the lock — so it still refuses, but only after queuing — also fails it, which is what makes it a test of \"before chaining\" rather than merely of \"returns an error\". Reverting either exhaustion site to an anonymous error fails the corresponding test.\n\nTwo things the mutation testing changed rather than confirmed. The first version of the capacity test hung under mutation instead of failing, so a broken implementation would have produced a ten-minute CI timeout rather than a diagnostic; it now asserts that the refusal is prompt, which is what \"cheaply\" means. And I had changed a second exhaustion site inside Rotate that no test covered, so I added one that reaches that line directly with a negative retry budget rather than leaving the change unproven.\n\nScope, stated so the reviewer can disagree: I did not document MaxQueueDepth in docs/reference/limits.md, because no flag or configuration surface reaches it, exactly like the existing undocumented MaxRetries, and kernel.Options is constructed in one place. I also did not set a default in the resident, since unbounded is current behaviour and changing it is beyond this request.\n\nPart one of the request remains blocked on hugh's ruling in bf399575; part three, the over-ceiling attachment path, is untouched and still to come.","body":{"commit":"f116097aacda9aee74ccdac0d6d762f9fe900f2f","path":"internal/kernel"}}